## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to wire config-driven per-tenant controls in a multi-DC docker-dca setup, with TLS edge and simple runtime enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config for runtime limits
- Per-tenant rate limiting (token bucket)
- TLS via Traefik edge proxy
- Basic cross-DC deployment considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: token bucket per tenant with 60 rpm
const limits = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('/etc/config/tenant-rate-limits.json','utf8'));
const buckets = new Map();
function allow(tenant){
  const now = Date.now();
  let b = buckets.get(tenant);
  if(!b){ b = {tokens: limits[tenant]||0, last: now}; buckets.set(tenant,b); }
  // simplistic: refill 1 token per second for 60s window
  const elapsed = (now - b.last)/1000; b.last = now; b.tokens = Math.min((limits[tenant]||0), b.tokens + elapsed);
  if(b.tokens >= 1){ b.tokens--; return true; }
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you safely refresh Docker Config without restarting all containers?
- What edge cases arise with clock skew or config changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic, per-tenant tracing in a multi-DC docker-dca setup, including config retrieval, TTL-based caching, and graceful degradation when the sidecar cannot fetch config.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry sampling and per-tenant configuration
- Consul KV as a central per-tenant policy store
- TTL caching to limit external calls
- Graceful fallback to sensible default rate
- Cross-DC deployment considerations and observability impact

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-sampler for per-tenant tracing
class TenantSampler {
  constructor(fetchRate) {
    this.fetchRate = fetchRate; // function(tenant) -> rate
    this.cache = new Map(); // tenant -> {rate, expiry}
  }
  getRate(tenant) {
    const entry = this.cache.get(tenant);
    if (entry && entry.expiry > Date.now()) return entry.rate;
    // fetch from Consul KV here, then cache for 5s
    const rate = this.fetchRate(tenant) ?? 20;
    this.cache.set(tenant, { rate, expiry: Date.now() + 5000 });
    return rate;
  }
  shouldSample(tenant) {
    const rate = this.getRate(tenant);
    return Math.random() * 100 < rate;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test correctness of per-tenant rates during rollout?
- What are failure modes if Consul KV is unavailable, and how to mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of per-tenant routing, docker configs, and zero-downtime reconfiguration in a multi-DC setup.

## Key Concepts
- Traefik dynamic config and weighted routing
- Docker Configs for per-tenant policy
- TLS termination at edge proxy
- Multi-DC docker-dca deployment basics
- Observability of per-tenant routing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Traefik dynamic config snippet (example)
{
  "http": {
    "routers": {
      "tenant-ab": {
        "rule": "Host(`api.local`)",
        "tls": { "certResolver": "myresolver" },
        "service": "ab-backend"
      }
    },
    "services": {
      "ab-backend": {
        "loadBalancer": {
          "weighted": {
            "services": [
              { "name": "api-v1", "weight": 80 },
              { "name": "api-v2", "weight": 20 }
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test per-tenant weight changes in production?
- How would you handle missing or malformed ab-split entries?
- How would you monitor skew and rollback strategies?
